1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,962
For my presentation today I want to tell you about how groups of elephants have been moved

2
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:09,550
and settled in new reserves.

3
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:15,982
This is known as translocation and has been carried out in Malawi in Africa

4
00:00:15,982 --> 00:00:17,670
in recent years.

5
00:00:17,670 --> 00:00:23,613
The reason this is being done is because of overpopulation of elephants in some

6
00:00:23,613 --> 00:00:25,260
areas.

7
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:31,232
Overpopulation is a good problem to have and not one we tend to hear

8
00:00:31,232 --> 00:00:32,740
about very often.

9
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:40,409
In Malawi’s Majete National Park the elephant population had been wiped out by

10
00:00:40,409 --> 00:00:41,051
poachers,

11
00:00:41,051 --> 00:00:43,500
who killed the elephants for their ivory.

12
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:51,840
But in 2003, the park was restocked and effective law enforcement was introduced.

13
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:56,680
Since then, not a single elephant has been poached.

14
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:02,200
In this safe environment, the elephant population boomed.

15
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:09,250
Breeding went so well that there were more elephants than the park could support.

16
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:12,570
This led to a number of problems.

17
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:16,427
Firstly, there was more competition for food,

18
00:01:16,427 --> 00:01:21,830
which meant that some elephants were suffering from hunger.

19
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:26,652
As there was a limit to the amount of food in the national park,

20
00:01:26,652 --> 00:01:30,550
some elephants began looking further afield.

21
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:36,430
Elephants were routinely knocking down fences around the park,

22
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:42,210
which then had to be repaired at a significant cost.

23
00:01:42,210 --> 00:01:48,368
To solve this problem, the decision was made to move dozens of elephants from

24
00:01:48,368 --> 00:01:55,710
Majete National Park to Nkhotakota Wildlife Park, where there were no elephants.

25
00:01:55,710 --> 00:02:02,584
But, obviously, attempting to move significant numbers of elephants to a new home 300

26
00:02:02,584 --> 00:02:10,580
kilometres away is quite a challenge.

27
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:16,870
So how did this translocation process work in practice?

28
00:02:16,870 --> 00:02:23,975
Elephants were moved in groups of between eight and twenty, all belonging to one

29
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:25,480
family.

30
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:32,293
Because relationships are very important to elephants, they all had to be moved at

31
00:02:32,293 --> 00:02:33,700
the same time.

32
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:39,716
A team of vets and park rangers flew over the park in helicopters

33
00:02:39,716 --> 00:02:42,283
and targeted a group,

34
00:02:42,283 --> 00:02:48,560
which were rounded up and directed to a designated open plain.

35
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:54,950
The vets then used darts to immobilise the elephants – this was a tricky

36
00:02:54,950 --> 00:03:02,622
manoeuvre, as they not only had to select the right dose of tranquiliser for different-sized elephants

37
00:03:02,622 --> 00:03:08,050
but they had to dart the elephants as they were running around.

38
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:12,505
This also had to be done as quickly as possible

39
00:03:12,505 --> 00:03:17,120
so as to minimise the stress caused.

40
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:22,387
As soon as the elephants began to flop onto the ground, the team moved

41
00:03:22,387 --> 00:03:24,900
in to take care of them.

42
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:30,213
To avoid the risk of suffocation, the team had to make sure none of

43
00:03:30,213 --> 00:03:36,288
the elephants were lying on their chests because their lungs could be crushed in

44
00:03:36,288 --> 00:03:37,310
this position.

45
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:43,000
So all the elephants had to be placed on their sides.

46
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,094
One person stayed with each elephant while they waited for the vets to do

47
00:03:49,094 --> 00:03:50,220
checks.

48
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:56,097
It was very important to keep an eye on their breathing – if there

49
00:03:56,097 --> 00:04:03,900
were fewer than six breaths per minute, the elephant would need urgent medical attention.

50
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:09,688
Collars were fitted to the matriarch in each group so their movements could be

51
00:04:09,688 --> 00:04:12,060
tracked in their new home.

52
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:19,737
Measurements were taken of each elephant’s tusks – elephants with large tusks would be

53
00:04:19,737 --> 00:04:25,430
at greater risk from poachers – and also of their feet.

54
00:04:25,430 --> 00:04:32,771
The elephants were then taken to a recovery area before being loaded onto trucks

55
00:04:32,771 --> 00:04:35,720
and transported to their new home.

56
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:44,097
The elephants translocated to Nkhotakota settled in very well and the project has generally

57
00:04:44,097 --> 00:04:50,930
been accepted to have been a huge success – and not just for the elephants.

58
00:04:50,930 --> 00:05:00,760
Employment prospects have improved enormously, contributing to rising living standards for the whole community.

59
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:07,402
Poaching is no longer an issue, as former poachers are able to find more

60
00:05:07,402 --> 00:05:09,710
reliable sources of income.

61
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:15,210
In fact, many of them volunteered to give up their weapons, as they were

62
00:05:15,210 --> 00:05:17,920
no longer of any use to them.

63
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:25,330
More than two dozen elephants have been born at Nkhotakota since relocation.

64
00:05:25,330 --> 00:05:32,251
With an area of more than 1,800 square kilometres, there’s plenty of space for

65
00:05:32,251 --> 00:05:35,020
the elephant population to continue to grow.

66
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:42,572
Their presence is also helping to rebalance Nkhotakota’s damaged ecosystem

67
00:05:42,572 --> 00:05:46,017
and providing a sustainable conservation model,

68
00:05:46,017 --> 00:05:54,390
which could be replicated in other parks.

69
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:55,413
All this has been a big draw for tourism,

70
00:05:55,413 --> 00:06:00,088
which contributes five times more than the illegal wildlife trade to GDP,

71
00:06:00,088 --> 00:06:03,880
and this is mainly because of the elephants.

72
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:08,370
There’s also been a dramatic rise in interest …
